{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically defining a lottery as right-skewed when a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., lower payoffs) compared to favorable ones. In this problem, both lotteries have a 0 Euro outcome with high probability (67% for B, 66% for A), and a positive outcome (2500 vs. 2400 Euros) with the remaining probability (33% vs. 34%). Since both lotteries have a large probability mass on the 0 Euro outcome (unfavorable), and a small mass on a high positive outcome (favorable), we must assess whether one is more right-skewed than the other. A right-skewed lottery would have more weight on the unfavorable (0 Euro) outcome. Here, B has a slightly higher probability (33% vs. 34%) of the favorable outcome, and a slightly lower probability (67% vs. 66%) of the unfavorable outcome. Thus, A has a slightly higher probability of the favorable outcome and a slightly lower probability of the unfavorable outcome. Therefore, B has a slightly greater weight on the unfavorable outcome (67% vs. 66%), making B more right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which would be B. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be applied to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: 'Lotterie B bietet mit einer um 1% verringerten Chance als Lotterie A 100€ mehr, dieses minimale Risiko mit einem doch bedeutend höheren Gewinn, würde ich eingehen.' This translates to: 'Lottery B offers 100€ more with a 1% reduced chance compared to Lottery A; despite this minimal risk, I would enter because of the significantly higher reward.' The individual acknowledges that B has a slightly lower probability (33% vs. 34%) of winning, which corresponds to a higher probability of getting nothing (67% vs. 66%). The individual highlights the higher payoff (2500€ vs. 2400€) and justifies choosing B due to the significantly higher reward, even with a small increase in risk (i.e., lower chance of winning). The deliberation focuses on the magnitude of the gain (100€ more) and the small reduction in probability, and the individual frames the decision around the higher potential gain. There is no mention of skewness, unfavorable outcomes, or any evaluation of the distribution of probabilities in terms of right-skewness. The justification is based on the size of the gain and the minimal risk, not on the structure of skewness.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is that Lottery B has a slightly higher probability (67%) of the unfavorable outcome (0€) compared to A (66%), making B more right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery (B). However, in the verbal report, the individual does not mention skewness, right-skewness, or any evaluation of the distribution of outcomes in terms of unfavorable vs. favorable probabilities. Instead, the individual justifies the choice based on the higher payoff and the minimal risk reduction. The justification is not aligned with the rule of the decision reason (preferring the more right-skewed lottery). Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason in their deliberation. The reported reasoning is based on gain size and risk magnitude, not on the skewness of the outcomes. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

